the abstract lemma: for any two distinct alignments a1 != a2, and any byte
count n that is a common multiple of both, a size-n type at align a1 and a
size-n type at align a2 have equal size and unequal alignment, hence are
different types. checked at three pairs, none matching arvo's 1-and-16.

pair 1: align(4,8) size  8 vs  8  align  4 vs  8
pair 2: align(2,32) size 32 vs 32  align  2 vs 32
pair 3: align(1,4) size  4 vs  4  align  1 vs  4

all three pairs compile and const-check: equal size, unequal alignment, at
byte counts and alignments that share nothing with arvo's WideBits<BYTES> /
AlignedWideBits16<BYTES> shape. the mechanism is general.

what this probe does NOT establish: that arvo's four strategies actually pick
two different alignments anywhere. that is a design choice, not a fact about
type systems, and per 15:418-429 and 15:553-556 it is stated as an open,
replaceable assumption in this panel's own source, not as settled design.
